    VALUES (subj, tid, 'The value of $\displaystyle\int{-\infty}^{+\infty} e^{-t}\,\delta(2t - 2)\,dt$, where $\delta(t)$ is the Dirac delta function, is', 'The value of $\displaystyle\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} e^{-t}\,\delta(2t - 2)\,dt$, where $\delta(t)$ is the Dirac delta function, is
', '**Idea used:** scaling of the delta function, $\delta(at) = \dfrac{1}{|a|}\delta(t)$, then the sifting property
$\int f(t)\,\delta(t - t_0)\,dt = f(t_0)$.

1. $\delta(2t - 2) = \delta\big(2(t - 1)\big) = \tfrac12\,\delta(t - 1)$.
2. The integral becomes $\tfrac12 \int e^{-t}\,\delta(t - 1)\,dt = \tfrac12\,e^{-1}$.

So the value is $\dfrac{1}{2e}$.

**Without a calculator:** the spike sits where $2t - 2 = 0$, i.e. $t = 1$, so the answer carries $e^{-1}$ (out go C and D).
Squeezing time by 2 makes the spike''s area half, so $\tfrac12$, not 2.

**Answer: (A).**
