    INSERT INTO question(subject_id, topic_id, summary, stem_md, solution_md, status, answer_type, nat_min, nat_max,
                         difficulty, answer_status, content_key, origin)
    VALUES (subj, tid, 'In the circuit shown below, the supply voltage is $10\sin(1000t)$ volts. The peak value of the steady state current through the 1 Ω resistor, in amperes, is .', 'In the circuit shown below, the supply voltage is $10\sin(1000t)$ volts. The peak value of the steady state current through the 1 Ω
resistor, in amperes, is ______.

![From the left rail: 4 ohm to a top node P, and a 250 microfarad parallel 4 mH tank to a lower node Q. 1 ohm from P to Q. From P: a 2 microfarad parallel 500 mH tank to the right rail. From Q: 5 ohm to the right rail. The source 10 sin(1000t) joins the two rails at the bottom](fig:q-gate2016-s1-q52)
', '**Idea used:** a parallel L-C tank at its resonant frequency has infinite impedance (an open circuit). Check each tank at
$\omega = 1000$ rad/s.

1. Lower tank: $X_L = 1000 \times 4 \times 10^{-3} = 4\ \Omega$; $X_C = \dfrac{1}{1000 \times 250 \times 10^{-6}} = 4\ \Omega$. Equal, so it is **open**.
2. Upper tank: $X_L = 1000 \times 0.5 = 500\ \Omega$; $X_C = \dfrac{1}{1000 \times 2 \times 10^{-6}} = 500\ \Omega$. Equal, so it is **open** too.
3. Only one path is left: 4 Ω → 1 Ω → 5 Ω, total 10 Ω.
4. Peak current $= 10 \div 10 = 1$ A, and all of it flows through the 1 Ω.

**Without a calculator:** whenever a paper gives "odd" L and C values with a round $\omega$, check $\omega^2 LC = 1$ first:
$10^6 \times 4 \times 10^{-3} \times 250 \times 10^{-6} = 1$ and $10^6 \times 0.5 \times 2 \times 10^{-6} = 1$.

**Answer: 1 A.**
